✅ Types of Build Triggers (All with Explanation + Example)

🔹 1. Manual Trigger
👉 What it is:
User starts the job manually from Jenkins UI

👉 When used:
Testing pipelines
One-time execution

👉 Example:
Click “Build Now” button

🔹 2. SCM Trigger (Polling)
👉 What it is:
Jenkins periodically checks Git/SVN repo for changes

👉 How it works:
Runs every defined interval
If changes detected → triggers build

👉 Example:
H/5 * * * * → check every 5 minutes

👉 Drawback:
Delay in triggering
Unnecessary polling load


🔹 3. Webhook Trigger (Best Practice ✅)
👉 What it is:
Git automatically notifies Jenkins on code change

👉 How it works:
Developer commits → Git sends webhook → Jenkins starts job

👉 Example:
GitHub webhook → http://jenkins-url/github-webhook/

👉 Advantage:
Instant trigger
No polling overhead


🔹 4. Scheduled Trigger (Cron Job)
👉 What it is:
Run job at specific time

👉 Example:
Daily backup job
Night build

👉 Cron Example:
0 2 * * * → run at 2 AM daily

🔹 5. Upstream/Downstream Trigger
👉 What it is:
One job triggers another job

👉 Example:
Build Job → triggers → Test Job

👉 Use case:
CI/CD chaining flow


🔹 6. Trigger via API (Remote Trigger)
👉 What it is:
Trigger job via HTTP API call

👉 Example:
curl http://jenkins/job/build?token=abc

👉 Use case:
External systems trigger Jenkins
Integration with tools

🔹 7. Parameterized Build Trigger
👉 What it is:
Trigger job with parameters

👉 Example:
ENV=dev
ACTION=deploy

👉 Use case:
Reusable pipelines


🔹 8. GitHub Pull Request Trigger
👉 What it is:
Trigger build when PR is opened/updated

👉 Example:
PR → run tests before merge

👉 Use case:
Code review validation


🔹 9. Timer + Poll SCM Combined
👉 What it is:
Schedule + SCM check together

👉 Example:
Run job only at fixed time and check repo

🔹 10. Build After Other Projects Are Built
👉 What it is:
Trigger job after another job finishes

👉 Condition:
Only on success / failure


